# This file has two jobs, and the second one matters more than the first.
#
#   1. Keep build output and IDE clutter out of the repository.
#   2. Keep out anything that identifies a person - whoever built it, and whoever
#      flew on the server. Those entries are marked below; they are not the kind of
#      mistake you notice after the fact, because a commit is permanent.
#
# NOT covered by any .gitignore, and worth knowing: the author name and e-mail in
# every commit. Set a noreply address (git config user.email
# "<id>+<handle>@users.noreply.github.com") and turn on "Keep my email addresses
# private" plus "Block command line pushes that expose my email" on GitHub. A
# .gitignore cannot help with that, and a rewrite afterwards is painful.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The bot's own runtime data - PRIVACY CRITICAL
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not just clutter. Each of these holds either a secret of yours or personal data
# belonging to other people, and the bot creates them next to its executable.

# Holds GeminiApiKey, DcsGrpcApiKey and DiscordWebhookUrl in plain text.
config.json

# Edited per installation; a committed copy would also overwrite somebody else's.
phrases.json
vocabulary.json

# One WAV per transmission: the recorded VOICES of other players, with their SRS
# names in the file name. The single worst thing in this project to commit.
recordings/

# Transcripts of what pilots said, plus their names and positions.
logs/

# Timestamped copies of all of the above.
Backup/

# Mission data saved from the GUI's explorer: unit names, player names, positions.
grpc-dumps/

# The speech model. Hundreds of MB, downloaded separately, not ours to redistribute.
models/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build output
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[Bb]in/
[Oo]bj/
publish/
[Dd]ebug/
[Rr]elease/
TestResults/
*.binlog

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Visual Studio and other editors - machine-specific, some of it identifying
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Several of these embed absolute paths from the machine they were written on,
# which is how a Windows user name ends up in a public repository.

.vs/
*.user
*.suo
*.userosscache
*.userprefs
*.rsuser
*.sln.docstates
*.sln.iml

# Publish profiles: local output paths, sometimes credentials.
*.pubxml
*.pubxml.user
*.publishproj
PublishScripts/

# Editor history can hold earlier versions of files you later cleaned up.
.history/
**/.localhistory/
.vscode/
!.vscode/extensions.json

# ReSharper / Rider / profiler caches - full of absolute paths.
_ReSharper*/
*.[Rr]e[Ss]harper
*.DotSettings.user
.idea/
.dotCover
*.psess
*.vsp
*.vspx
*.coverage
*.coveragexml

# Local secrets, if any ever get created here. User secrets normally live in
# %APPDATA% rather than the repository, but a stray copy must not be committed.
*.env
**/secrets.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Installer build artifacts (produced by build-installer.ps1)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
installer/publish/
installer/output/
installer/vosk-model/

# The downloaded dependency bootstrappers - vendors' redistributables, several
# hundred MB, and re-downloaded by the build script when missing.
installer/*.exe

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Operating system droppings
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
Desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/
.DS_Store
.DS_Store?
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
